 same sample mean carries far more weight when it comes from a larger sample."},{"correct":false,"text":"Do not reject $H_0—the$ results are still not significant","feedback":"0.006 is much smaller than 0.05, so the results ARE significant."},{"correct":false,"text":"The conclusion is unrelated to the sample size","feedback":"The data did not change in direction—the increased sample size is what strengthened the evidence."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Compare with the $n = 4$ case, where the same sample mean gave a p-value of 0.159.","explanation":""},"key":"la76rAzV8v"}],"key":"YcnaxYeomW"}],"key":"AEc1EIMB0M"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Step 3: Finding the P-value for a Mean","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-3-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"Tests and Confidence Intervals for a Mean","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-5-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}